darray\n1263 |         Input array.\n1264 |     axis : int\n1265 |         The axis to be rolled. The positions of the other axes do not\n1266 |         change relative to one another.\n1267 |     start : int, optional\n1268 |         When ``start <= axis``, the axis is rolled back until it lies in\n1269 |         this position. When ``start > axis``, the axis is rolled until it\n1270 |         lies before this position. The default, 0, results in a \"complete\"\n1271 |         roll. The following table describes how negative values of ``start``\n1272 |         are interpreted:\n1273 | \n1274 |         .. table::\n1275 |            :align: left\n1276 | \n1277 |            +-------------------+----------------------+\n1278 |            |     ``start``     | Normalized ``start`` |\n1279 |            +===================+======================+\n1280 |            | ``-(arr.ndim+1)`` | raise ``AxisError``  |\n1281 |            +-------------------+----------------------+\n1282 |            | ``-arr.ndim``     | 0                    |\n1283 |            +-------------------+----------------------+\n1284 |            | |vdots|           | |vdots|              |\n1285 |            +-------------------+----------------------+\n1286 |            | ``-1``            | ``arr.ndim-1``       |\n1287 |            +-------------------+----------------------+\n1288 |            | ``0``             | ``0``                |\n1289 |            +-------------------+----------------------+\n1290 |            | |vdots|           | |vdots|              |\n1291 |            +-------------------+----------------------+\n1292 |            | ``arr.ndim``      | ``arr.ndim``         |\n1293 |            +-------------------+----------------------+\n1294 |            | ``arr.ndim + 1``  | raise ``AxisError``  |\n1295 |            +-------------------+----------------------+\n1296 |            \n1297 |         .. |vdots|   unicode:: U+22EE .. Vertical Ellipsis\n1298 | \n1299 |     Returns\n1300 |     -------\n1301 |     res : ndarray\n1302 |         For NumPy >= 1.10.0 a view of `a` is always returned. For earlier\n1303 |         NumPy versions a view of `a` is returned only if the order of the\n1304 |         axes is changed, otherwise the input array is returned.\n1305 | \n1306 |     See Also\n1307 |     --------\n1308 |     moveaxis : Move array axes to new positions.\n1309 |     roll : Roll the elements of an array by a number of positions along a\n1310 |         given axis.\n1311 | \n1312 |     Examples\n1313 |     --------\n1314 |     >>> a = np.ones((3,4,5,6))\n1315 |     >>> np.rollaxis(a, 3, 1).shape\n1316 |     (3, 6, 4, 5)\n1317 |     >>> np.rollaxis(a, 2).shape\n1318 |     (5, 3, 4, 6)\n1319 |     >>> np.rollaxis(a, 1, 4).shape\n1320 |     (3, 5, 6, 4)\n1321 | \n1322 |     \"\"\"\n1323 |     n = a.ndim\n1324 |     axis = normalize_axis_index(axis, n)\n1325 |     if start < 0:\n1326 |         start += n\n1327 |     msg = \"'%s' arg requires %d <= %s < %d, but %d was passed in\"\n1328 |     if not (0 <= start < n + 1):\n1329 |         raise AxisError(msg % ('start', -n, 'start', n + 1, start))\n1330 |     if axis < start:\n1331 |         # it's been removed\n1332 |         start -= 1\n1333 |     if axis == start:\n1334 |         return a[...]\n1335 |     axes = list(range(0, n))\n1336 |     axes.remove(axis)\n1337 |     axes.insert(start, axis)\n1338 |     return a.transpose(axes)\n```\n\nFound function `numpy/core/multiarray.py:busday_offset`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1447 | def busday_offset(dates, offsets, roll=None, weekmask=None, holidays=None,\n1448 |                   busdaycal=None, out=None):\n1449 |     \"\"\"\n1450 |     busday_offset(dates, offsets, roll='raise', weekmask='1111100', holidays=None, busdaycal=None, out=None)\n1451 | \n1452 |     First adjusts the date to fall on a valid day according to\n1453 |     the ``roll`` rule, then applies offsets to the given dates\n1454 |     counted in valid days.\n1455 | \n1456 |     .. versionadded:: 1.7.0\n1457 | \n1458 |     Parameters\n1459 |     ----------\n1460 |     dates : array_like of datetime64[D]\n1461 |         The array of dates to process.\n1462 |     offsets : array_like of int\n1463 |         The array of offsets, which is broadcast with ``dates``.\n1464 |     roll : {'raise', 'nat', 'forward', 'following', 'backward', 'preceding', 'modifiedfollowing', 'modifiedpreceding'}, optional\n1465 |         How to treat dates that do not fall on a valid day. The default\n1466 |         is 'raise'.\n1467 | \n1468 |           * 'raise' means to raise an exception for an invalid day.\n1469 |           * 'nat' means to return a NaT (not-a-time) for an invalid day.\n1470 |           * 'forward' and 'following' mean to take the first valid day\n1471 |             later in time.\n1472 |           * 'backward' and 'preceding' mean to take the first valid day\n1473 |             earlier in time.\n1474 |           * 'modifiedfollowing' means to take the first valid day\n1475 |             later in time unless it is across a Month boundary, in which\n1476 |             case to take the first valid day earlier in time.\n1477 |           * 'modifiedpreceding' means to take the first valid day\n1478 |             earlier in time unless it is across a Month boundary, in which\n1479 |             case to take the first valid day later in time.\n1480 |     weekmask : str or array_like of bool, optional\n1481 |         A seven-element array indicating which of Monday through Sunday are\n1482 |         valid days. May be specified as a length-seven list or array, like\n1483 |         [1,1,1,1,1,0,0]; a length-seven string, like '1111100'; or a string\n1484 |         like \"Mon Tue Wed Thu Fri\", made up of 3-character abbreviations for\n1485 |         weekdays, optionally separated by white space. Valid abbreviations\n1486 |         are: Mon Tue Wed Thu Fri Sat Sun\n1487 |     holidays : array_like of datetime64[D], optional\n1488 |         An array of dates to consider as invalid dates.  They may be\n1489 |         specified in any order, and NaT (not-a-time) dates are ignored.\n1490 |         This list is saved in a normalized form that is suited for\n1491 |         fast calculations of valid days.\n1492 |     busdaycal : busdaycalendar, optional\n1493 |         A `busdaycalendar` object which specifies the valid days. If this\n1494 |         parameter is provided, neither weekmask nor holidays may be\n1495 |         provided.\n1496 |     out : array of datetime64[D], optional\n1497 |         If provided, this array is filled with the result.\n1498 | \n1499 |     Returns\n1500 |     -------\n1501 |     out : array of datetime64[D]\n1502 |         An array with a shape from broadcasting ``dates`` and ``offsets``\n1503 |         together, containing the dates with offsets applied.\n1504 | \n1505 |     See Also\n1506 |     --------\n1507 |     busdaycalendar: An object that specifies a custom set of valid days.\n1508 |     is_busday : Returns a boolean array indicating valid days.\n1509 |     busday_count : Counts how many valid days are in a half-open date range.\n1510 | \n1511 |     Examples\n1512 |     --------\n1513 |     >>> # First business day in October 2011 (not accounting for holidays)\n1514 |     ... np.busday_offset('2011-10', 0, roll='forward')\n1515 |     numpy.datetime64('2011-10-03')\n1516 |     >>> # Last business day in February 2012 (not accounting for holidays)\n1517 |     ... np.busday_offset('2012-03', -1, roll='forward')\n1518 |     numpy.datetime64('2012-02-29')\n1519 |     >>> # Third Wednesday in January 2011\n1520 |     ... np.busday_offset('2011-01', 2, roll='forward', weekmask='Wed')\n1521 |     numpy.datetime64('2011-01-19')\n1522 |     >>> # 2012 Mother's Day in Canada and the U.S.\n1523 |     ... np.busday_offset('2012-05', 1, roll='forward', weekmask='Sun')\n1524 |     numpy.datetime64('2012-05-13')\n1525 | \n1526 |     >>> # First business day on or after a date\n1527 |     ... np.busday_offset('2011-03-20', 0, roll='forward')\n1528 |     numpy.datetime64('2011-03-21')\n1529 |     >>> np.busday_offset('2011-03-22', 0, roll='forward')\n1530 |     numpy.datetime64('2011-03-22')\n1531 |     >>> # First business day after a date\n1532 |     ... np.busday_offset('2011-03-20', 1, roll='backward')\n1533 |     numpy.datetime64('2011-03-21')\n1534 |     >>> np.busday_offset('2011-03-22', 1, roll='backward')\n1535 |     numpy.datetime64('2011-03-23')\n1536 |     \"\"\"\n1537 |     return (dates, offsets, weekmask, holidays, out)\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"stride\", \"sliding window rolling window stride\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `numpy/core/_internal.py:_ctypes.strides`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n334 | def strides(self):\n335 |         \"\"\"\n336 |         (c_intp*self.ndim): A ctypes array of length self.ndim where\n337 |         the basetype is the same as for the shape attribute. This ctypes array\n338 |         contains the strides information from the underlying array. This strides\n339 |         information is important for showing how many bytes must be jumped to\n340 |         get to the next element in the array.\n341 |         \"\"\"\n342 |         return self.strides_as(_getintp_ctype())\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"stride\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `benchmarks/benchmarks/bench_avx.py:AVX_UFunc`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n25 | class AVX_UFunc(Benchmark):\n26 |     params = [avx_ufuncs, stride, dtype]\n27 |     param_names = ['avx_based_ufunc', 'stride', 'dtype']\n28 |     timeout = 10\n29 | \n30 |     def setup(self, ufuncname, stride, dtype):\n31 |         np.seterr(all='ignore')\n32 |         try:\n33 |             self.f = getattr(np, ufuncname)\n34 |         except AttributeError:\n35 |             raise NotImplementedError()\n36 |         N = 10000\n37 |         self.arr = np.ones(stride*N, 