n_samples, n_outputs)\n 162 |         Estimated target values.\n 163 | \n 164 |     sample_weight : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default=None\n 165 |         Sample weights.\n 166 | \n 167 |     multioutput : {'raw_values', 'uniform_average'}  or array-like of shape \\\n 168 |             (n_outputs,), default='uniform_average'\n 169 |         Defines aggregating of multiple output values.\n 170 |         Array-like value defines weights used to average errors.\n 171 | \n 172 |         'raw_values' :\n 173 |             Returns a full set of errors in case of multioutput input.\n 174 | \n 175 |         'uniform_average' :\n 176 |             Errors of all outputs are averaged with uniform weight.\n 177 | \n 178 |     Returns\n 179 |     -------\n 180 |     loss : float or array of floats\n 181 |         If multioutput is 'raw_values', then mean absolute error is returned\n 182 |         for each output separately.\n 183 |         If multioutput is 'uniform_average' or an ndarray of weights, then the\n 184 |         weighted average of all output errors is returned.\n 185 | \n 186 |         MAE output is non-negative floating point. The best value is 0.0.\n 187 | \n 188 |     Examples\n 189 |     --------\n 190 |     >>> from sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error\n 191 |     >>> y_true = [3, -0.5, 2, 7]\n 192 |     >>> y_pred = [2.5, 0.0, 2, 8]\n 193 |     >>> mean_absolute_error(y_true, y_pred)\n 194 |     0.5\n 195 |     >>> y_true = [[0.5, 1], [-1, 1], [7, -6]]\n 196 |     >>> y_pred = [[0, 2], [-1, 2], [8, -5]]\n 197 |     >>> mean_absolute_error(y_true, y_pred)\n 198 |     0.75\n 199 |     >>> mean_absolute_error(y_true, y_pred, multioutput='raw_values')\n 200 |     array([0.5, 1. ])\n 201 |     >>> mean_absolute_error(y_true, y_pred, multioutput=[0.3, 0.7])\n 202 |     0.85...\n 203 |     \"\"\"\n 204 |     input_arrays = [y_true, y_pred, sample_weight, multioutput]\n 205 |     xp, _ = get_namespace(*input_arrays)\n 206 | \n 207 |     dtype = _find_ma