atch found for entity name `pandas/io/excel/_base.py:ExcelWriter`.\n```\n 955 | class ExcelWriter(Generic[_WorkbookT]):\n 956 |     \"\"\"\n 957 |     Class for writing DataFrame objects into excel sheets.\n 958 | \n 959 |     Default is to use:\n 960 | \n 961 |     * `xlsxwriter <https://pypi.org/project/XlsxWriter/>`__ for xlsx files if xlsxwriter\n 962 |       is installed otherwise `openpyxl <https://pypi.org/project/openpyxl/>`__\n 963 |     * `odf <https://pypi.org/project/odfpy/>`__ for ods files\n 964 | \n 965 |     See :meth:`DataFrame.to_excel` for typical usage.\n 966 | \n 967 |     The writer should be used as a context manager. Otherwise, call `close()` to save\n 968 |     and close any opened file handles.\n 969 | \n 970 |     Parameters\n 971 |     ----------\n 972 |     path : str or typing.BinaryIO\n 973 |         Path to xls or xlsx or ods file.\n 974 |     engine : str (optional)\n 975 |         Engine to use for writing. If None, defaults to\n 976 |         ``io.excel.<extension>.writer``.  NOTE: can only be passed as a keyword\n 977 |         argument.\n 978 |     date_format : str, default None\n 979 |         Format string for dates written into Excel files (e.g. 'YYYY-MM-DD').\n 980 |     datetime_format : str, default None\n 981 |         Format string for datetime objects written into Excel files.\n 982 |         (e.g. 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS').\n 983 |     mode : {{'w', 'a'}}, default 'w'\n 984 |         File mode to use (write or append). Append does not work with fsspec URLs.\n 985 |     {storage_options}\n 986 | \n 987 |     if_sheet_exists : {{'error', 'new', 'replace', 'overlay'}}, default 'error'\n 988 |         How to behave when trying to write to a sheet that already\n 989 |         exists (append mode only).\n 990 | \n 991 |         * error: raise a ValueError.\n 992 |         * new: Create a new sheet, with a name determined by the engine.\n 993 |         * replace: Delete the contents of the sheet before writing to it.\n 994 |         * overlay: Write contents to the existing sheet without first removing,\n 995 |           but possibly over top of, the existing contents.\n 996 | \n 997 |         .. versionadded:: 1.3.0\n 998 | \n 999 |         .. versionchanged:: 1.4.0\n1000 | \n1001 |            Added ``overlay`` option\n1002 | \n1003 |     engine_kwargs : dict, optional\n1004 |         Keyword arguments to be passed into the engine. These will be passed to\n1005 |         the following functions of the respective engines:\n1006 | \n1007 |         * xlsxwriter: ``xlsxwriter.Workbook(file, **engine_kwargs)``\n1008 |         * openpyxl (write mode): ``openpyxl.Workbook(**engine_kwargs)``\n1009 |         * openpyxl (append mode): ``openpyxl.load_workbook(file, **engine_kwargs)``\n1010 |         * odf: ``odf.opendocument.OpenDocumentSpreadsheet(**engine_kwargs)``\n1011 | \n1012 |         .. versionadded:: 1.3.0\n1013 | \n1014 |     See Also\n1015 |     --------\n1016 |     read_excel : Read an Excel sheet values (xlsx) file into DataFrame.\n1017 |     read_csv : Read a comma-separated values (csv) file into DataFrame.\n1018 |     read_fwf : Read a table of fixed-width formatted lines into DataFrame.\n1019 | \n1020 |     Notes\n1021 |     -----\n1022 |     For compatibility with CSV writers, ExcelWriter serializes lists\n1023 |     and dicts to strings before writing.\n1024 | \n1025 |     Examples\n1026 |     --------\n1027 |     Default usage:\n1028 | \n1029 |     >>> df = pd.DataFrame([[\"ABC\", \"XYZ\"]], columns=[\"Foo\", \"Bar\"])  # doctest: +SKIP\n1030 |     >>> with pd.ExcelWriter(\"path_to_file.xlsx\") as writer:\n1031 |     ...     df.to_excel(writer)  # doctest: +SKIP\n1032 | \n1033 |     To write to separate sheets in a single file:\n1034 | \n1035 |     >>> df1 = pd.DataFrame([[\"AAA\", \"BBB\"]], columns=[\"Spam\", \"Egg\"])  # doctest: +SKIP\n1036 |     >>> df2 = pd.DataFrame([[\"ABC\", \"XYZ\"]], columns=[\"Foo\", \"Bar\"])  # doctest: +SKIP\n1037 |     >>> with pd.ExcelWriter(\"path_to_file.xlsx\") as writer:\n1038 |     ...     df1.to_excel(writer, sheet_name=\"Sheet1\")  # doctest: +SKIP\n1039 |     ...     df2.to_excel(writer, sheet_name=\"Sheet2\")  # doctest: +SKIP\n1040 | \n1041 |     You can set the date format or datetime format:\n1042 | \n1043 |     >>> from datetime import date, datetime  # doctest: +SKIP\n1044 |     >>> df = pd.DataFrame(\n1045 |     ...     [\n1046 |     ...         [date(2014, 1, 31), date(1999, 9, 24)],\n1047 |     ...         [datetime(1998, 5, 26, 23, 33, 4), datetime(2014, 2, 28, 13, 5, 13)],\n1048 |     ...     ],\n1049 |     ...     index=[\"Date\", \"Datetime\"],\n1050 |     ...     columns=[\"X\", \"Y\"],\n1051 |     ... )  # doctest: +SKIP\n1052 |     >>> with pd.ExcelWriter(\n1053 |     ...     \"path_to_file.xlsx\",\n1054 |     ...     date_format=\"YYYY-MM-DD\",\n1055 |     ...     datetime_format=\"YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS\",\n1056 |     ... ) as writer:\n1057 |     ...     df.to_excel(writer)  # doctest: +SKIP\n1058 | \n1059 |     You can also append to an existing Excel file:\n1060 | \n1061 |     >>> with pd.ExcelWriter(\"path_to_file.xlsx\", mode=\"a\", engine=\"openpyxl\") as writer:\n1062 |     ...     df.to_excel(writer, sheet_name=\"Sheet3\")  # doctest: +SKIP\n1063 | \n1064 |     Here, the `if_sheet_exists` parameter can be set to replace a sheet if it\n1065 |     already exists:\n1066 | \n1067 |     >>> with pd.ExcelWriter(\n1068 |     ...     \"path_to_file.xlsx\",\n1069 |     ...     mode=\"a\",\n1070 |     ...     engine=\"openpyxl\",\n1071 |     ...     if_sheet_exists=\"replace\",\n1072 |     ... ) as writer:\n1073 |     ...     df.to_excel(writer, sheet_name=\"Sheet1\")  # doctest: +SKIP\n1074 | \n1075 |     You can also write multiple DataFrames to a single sheet. Note that the\n1076 |     ``if_sheet_exists`` parameter needs to be set to ``overlay``:\n1077 | \n1078 |     >>> with pd.ExcelWriter(\n1079 |     ...     \"path_to_file.xlsx\",\n1080 |     ...     mode=\"a\",\n1081 |     ...   