ng existing values.\n798 |             * 'nearest', 'zero', 'slinear', 'quadratic', 'cubic',\n799 |               'barycentric', 'polynomial': Passed to\n800 |               `scipy.interpolate.interp1d`, whereas 'spline' is passed to\n801 |               `scipy.interpolate.UnivariateSpline`. These methods use the numerical\n802 |               values of the index.  Both 'polynomial' and 'spline' require that\n803 |               you also specify an `order` (int), e.g.\n804 |               ``df.interpolate(method='polynomial', order=5)``. Note that,\n805 |               `slinear` method in Pandas refers to the Scipy first order `spline`\n806 |               instead of Pandas first order `spline`.\n807 |             * 'krogh', 'piecewise_polynomial', 'spline', 'pchip', 'akima',\n808 |               'cubicspline': Wrappers around the SciPy interpolation methods of\n809 |               similar names. See `Notes`.\n810 |             * 'from_derivatives': Refers to\n811 |               `scipy.interpolate.BPoly.from_derivatives`.\n812 | \n813 |         axis : {{0 or 'index', 1 or 'columns', None}}, default None\n814 |             Axis to interpolate along. For `Series` this parameter is unused\n815 |             and defaults to 0.\n816 |         limit : int, optional\n817 |             Maximum number of consecutive NaNs to fill. Must be greater than\n818 |             0.\n819 |         inplace : bool, default False\n820 |             Update the data in place if possible.\n821 |         limit_direction : {{'forward', 'backward', 'both'}}, Optional\n822 |             Consecutive NaNs will be filled in this direction.\n823 | \n824 |         limit_area : {{`None`, 'inside', 'outside'}}, default None\n825 |             If limit is specified, consecutive NaNs will be filled with this\n826 |             restriction.\n827 | \n828 |             * ``None``: No fill restriction.\n829 |             * 'inside': Only fill NaNs surrounded by valid values\n830 |               (interpolate