iodIndex\n961 |         if is_period_index:\n962 |             return result_interpolated\n963 | \n964 |         # Make sure that original data points which do not align with the\n965 |         # resampled index are removed\n966 |         result_interpolated = result_interpolated.loc[final_index]\n967 | \n968 |         # Make sure frequency indexes are preserved\n969 |         result_interpolated.index = final_index\n970 |         return result_interpolated\n```\n\n\n##Searching for entity `pandas/core/arrays/base.py:ExtensionArray.interpolate`...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/core/arrays/base.py:ExtensionArray.interpolate`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `pandas/core/arrays/base.py:ExtensionArray.interpolate`.\n```\n 989 | def interpolate(\n 990 |         self,\n 991 |         *,\n 992 |         method: InterpolateOptions,\n 993 |         axis: int,\n 994 |         index: Index,\n 995 |         limit,\n 996 |         limit_direction,\n 997 |         limit_area,\n 998 |         copy: bool,\n 999 |         **kwargs,\n1000 |     ) -> Self:\n1001 |         \"\"\"\n1002 |         Fill NaN values using an interpolation method.\n1003 | \n1004 |         Parameters\n1005 |         ----------\n1006 |         method : str, default 'linear'\n1007 |             Interpolation technique to use. One of:\n1008 |             * 'linear': Ignore the index and treat the values as equally spaced.\n1009 |             This is the only method supported on MultiIndexes.\n1010 |             * 'time': Works on daily and higher resolution data to interpolate\n1011 |             given length of interval.\n1012 |             * 'index', 'values': use the actual numerical values of the index.\n1013 |             * 'nearest', 'zero', 'slinear', 'quadratic', 'cubic', 'barycentric',\n1014 |             'polynomial': Passed to scipy.interpolate.interp1d, whereas 'spline'\n1015 |             is passed to scipy.interpolate.UnivariateSpline. These methods use\n1016 |             the numerical values of the index.\n1017 |             Both 'polynomial' and 'spline' require that you also specify an\n1018 |             order (int), e.g. arr.interpolate(method='polynomial', order=5).\n1019 |             * 'krogh', 'piecewise_polynomial', 'spline', 'pchip', 'akima',\n1020 |             'cubicspline': Wrappers around the SciPy interpolation methods\n1021 |             of similar names. See Notes.\n1022 |             * 'from_derivatives': Refers to scipy.interpolate.BPoly.from_derivatives.\n1023 |         axis : int\n1024 |             Axis to interpolate along. For 1-dimensional data, use 0.\n1025 |         index : Index\n1026 |             Index to use for interpolation.\n1027 |         limit : int or None\n1028 |             Maximum number of consecutive NaNs to fill. Must be greater than 0.\n1029 |         limit_direction : {'forward', 'backward', 'both'}\n1030 |             Consecutive NaNs will be filled in this direction.\n1031 |         limit_area : {'inside', 'outside'} or None\n1032 |             If limit is specified, consecutive NaNs will be filled with this\n1033 |             restriction.\n1034 |             * None: No fill restriction.\n1035 |             * 'inside': Only fill NaNs surrounded by valid values (interpolate).\n1036 |             * 'outside': Only fill NaNs outside valid values (extrapolate).\n1037 |         copy : bool\n1038 |             If True, a copy of the object is returned with interpolated values.\n1039 |         **kwargs : optional\n1040 |             Keyword arguments to pass on to the interpolating function.\n1041 | \n1042 |         Returns\n1043 |         -------\n1044 |         ExtensionArray\n1045 |             An ExtensionArray with interpolated values.\n1046 | \n1047 |         See Also\n1048 |         --------\n1049 |         Series.interpolate : Interpolate values in a Series.\n1050 |         DataFrame.interpolate : Interpolate values in a DataFrame.\n1051 | \n1052 |         Notes\n1053 |         -----